Debugging the routing base path for the app's environment config.

Debugging the routing base path for the app's environment config.

Done

App.tsx is correct now: the imports, the absolute paths, and the <li> wrappers are all right.

main.tsx has one bug:

<BrowserRouter basename={import.meta.env.mp2}>

BASE_URL isn't a placeholder for you to replace. It's the literal name of a property Vite provides, and Vite fills it with whatever you set as base (/mp2/). There's no property called mp2, so this evaluates to undefined. The router then assumes your app lives at /, so on /mp2/ none of your routes match and the page shows nothing.

Change it to exactly import.meta.env.BASE_URL.

Why didn't TypeScript catch this? Vite types import.meta.env so that any property name is allowed, because you can define your own environment variables. TypeScript catches most mistakes, but not all of them. When something looks right but doesn't work, check places where anything is accepted.

Once that's fixed, confirm that both links work and that /mp2/apod/2026-09-15 shows "Detail View."

Step 5: Fetch the data once and share it

All three pages need the same pictures, and Previous/Next needs to know the order. So instead of each page fetching separately, App fetches once and passes the pictures down. This is called lifting state up: data lives in the closest component that contains everyone who needs it.

5a. A small change to api/apod.ts

Remove the endDate parameter and the end_date line. If you only send start_date, NASA fills in "today" using its own time zone. If your computer's "today" is ahead of NASA's, asking for a date it hasn't posted yet would cause an error.

5b. Fetch in App.tsx

Add this inside App, above the return:

const [pictures, setPictures] = useState<ApodPicture[]>([]);
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

useEffect(() => {
  const start = new Date();
  start.setDate(start.getDate() - 60);
  const startDate = start.toISOString().slice(0, 10);

  fetchPictures(startDate)
    .then((data) => setPictures(data))
    .catch(() => setError('Could not load pictures from NASA. Please try again later.'))
    .finally(() => setLoading(false));
}, []);

You'll need to import useState and useEffect from 'react', fetchPictures from './api/apod', and type ApodPicture from './types'.

Here's what's new:

Three pieces of state: the data, whether it's still loading, and an error message. string | null means "a message, or nothing if there's no error."

useEffect(() => { ... }, []) runs code after the component renders. The empty array [] at the end means "run this only once, when the component first appears." Without it, the effect would run after every render: fetch, set state, re-render, fetch again, forever.

The date math: today's date minus 60 days, formatted as YYYY-MM-DD, which is what NASA expects.

.then / .catch / .finally: on success, store the data; on failure, store a friendly message; either way, loading is over.

In development, you may see the request happen twice in your browser's Network tab. That's StrictMode deliberately running effects twice to expose bugs. It doesn't happen in the deployed build.

5c. Show loading and errors, then pass the data down (your job)

Before <Routes>, show a "Loading..." message while loading is true, and show error if it isn't null. In JSX, {condition && <p>...</p>} renders the paragraph only when the condition is true.

Pass the data into the list page: <ListView pictures={pictures} />.

In ListView.tsx, accept that prop. Define its type and receive it in the function's parameters:
interface ListViewProps {
  pictures: ApodPicture[];
}

export default function ListView({ pictures }: ListViewProps) {

Still in ListView, render one <li> per picture showing its date and title. Use .map() on the array, which turns each item into a piece of JSX:
<ul>
  {pictures.map((picture) => (
    <li key={...}>...</li>
  ))}
</ul>

Fill in the two blanks. For key, React needs a value that's unique for each item, so it can track which row is which when the list re-sorts or filters. Which field of an APOD picture is guaranteed unique?

When you see about 60 real NASA titles on your list page, paste your ListView.tsx. Then we'll add the search bar and sorting, which is the first 28 points of the rubric.

