Building a gallery view with filtering options.

Building a gallery view with filtering options.

Done

All four tests passing means the whole List View section of the rubric is done: that's 28 points, plus the list half of the detail-view clicking.

Commit and push:

git add .
git commit -m "Add search and sorting to list view"
git push

Step 7: Gallery view

We'll split this step into two parts: first get the images on screen, then add the filter.

7a. A helper for the image URL

Here's a wrinkle: for an image day, the picture is in url. But for a video day, url is a YouTube or video link, which can't go in an <img> tag. The image to use is thumbnail_url (that's why we asked for thumbs: true).

The detail page will need this logic too, so write it once in a shared file. Create src/utils.ts:

import type { ApodPicture } from './types';

export function getImageUrl(picture: ApodPicture): string | undefined {
  return picture.media_type === 'image' ? picture.url : picture.thumbnail_url;
}

The ? : is a ternary, a compact if/else: condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse. The return type is string | undefined because thumbnail_url is optional, so a video might not have one. TypeScript will make you deal with that case.

7b. The gallery grid (your job)

In GalleryView.tsx, follow the same pattern as ListView:

Accept pictures as a prop, with a GalleryViewProps interface. In App.tsx, pass it in on the gallery route, just as you did for the list.

Render a container <div>, and inside it, .map() over the pictures. For each picture, render a Link to its detail page, containing an <img>:
src comes from getImageUrl(picture).

alt is picture.title. Alt text is read by screen readers and shown if the image fails to load. Always include it.

Put the key on the Link, since it's now the outermost element in each item.

Skip pictures with no image. Before mapping, filter out pictures where getImageUrl(picture) is undefined. You already know .filter(), so this is one line.

7c. Style it with a CSS Module

Without styling, 60 full-size images would stack vertically down an enormous page. Since inline styles are banned, we'll use a CSS Module, which the assignment recommends.

A CSS Module is a CSS file whose class names only apply to the component that imports it. With a normal CSS file, a .grid class in the gallery could accidentally restyle something on another page. With a module, Vite renames each class to something unique behind the scenes, so classes can't collide.

Create src/pages/GalleryView.module.css:

.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr));
  gap: 12px;
}

.image {
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 1;
  object-fit: cover;
  display: block;
}

Here's what that does:

display: grid with repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr)): "fit as many columns as possible, each at least 200px wide, sharing any leftover space equally." That means the gallery automatically has more columns on a wide screen and fewer on a phone, with no extra code.

aspect-ratio: 1 with object-fit: cover: every tile is a square, and each image is cropped to fill its square rather than being stretched.

Use it in the component like this:

import styles from './GalleryView.module.css';
...
<div className={styles.grid}>

Note that JSX uses className, not class, because class is a reserved word in JavaScript. Apply styles.image to each <img> the same way.

Check your work

The Gallery page should show a grid of square images that adjusts its columns when you resize the window, and clicking any image should go to its detail URL.

Paste your GalleryView.tsx when it works. Then we'll add the filter.

