Building a month-based filter for the photo gallery.

Building a month-based filter for the photo gallery.

Done

Now the second gallery requirement: a filter where users can select one or many options.

Step 8: Gallery filter by month

The plan: find every month present in the data, show a toggle button for each, and show only the pictures from the selected months. If nothing is selected, show everything. That way the gallery is never empty by default.

Section 1: A month-label helper (add to src/utils.ts)

export function formatMonth(month: string): string {
  return new Date(`${month}-01T00:00:00`).toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
    month: 'long',
    year: 'numeric',
  });
}

This turns "2026-09" into "September 2026" for the button labels.

The T00:00:00 matters. Without a time, JavaScript reads "2026-09-01" as midnight in UTC (London time). In the US, that's still August 31st, so the button would wrongly say "August 2026." Adding a time makes JavaScript use your local time zone. Date bugs like this are extremely common, which is why it's worth knowing.

Section 2: New imports (top of GalleryView.tsx)

Add useState, and add formatMonth to the existing utils import:

import { useState } from 'react';
import { getImageUrl, formatMonth } from '../utils';

Section 3: The filter logic (Zone 2, after your withImages line)

const [selectedMonths, setSelectedMonths] = useState<string[]>([]);

  const months = [...new Set(withImages.map((picture) => picture.date.slice(0, 7)))].sort();

  function toggleMonth(month: string) {
    if (selectedMonths.includes(month)) {
      setSelectedMonths(selectedMonths.filter((m) => m !== month));
    } else {
      setSelectedMonths([...selectedMonths, month]);
    }
  }

  const visible =
    selectedMonths.length === 0
      ? withImages
      : withImages.filter((picture) => selectedMonths.includes(picture.date.slice(0, 7)));

Here's each piece:

selectedMonths is an array of strings like ["2026-08", "2026-09"]. An array is what makes "select many" possible.

months finds the months present, in three steps:
.map(... .slice(0, 7)) turns every date into its first 7 characters: "2026-09-15" becomes "2026-09". That gives about 60 months, with lots of repeats.

new Set(...) removes the duplicates. A Set is a collection that can't contain the same value twice.

[... ].sort() spreads the Set back into an array and sorts it, oldest month first.

toggleMonth adds a month if it isn't selected, and removes it if it is. Notice it never modifies selectedMonths directly (no .push()). It always builds a new array and passes it to the setter. React only re-renders when it receives a new value; if you changed the old array in place, React would see "same array as before" and skip the update.

visible is everything when nothing is selected, and otherwise only the pictures whose month is in the selection.

Section 4: The buttons and the grid (Zone 3)

Replace your return with:

return (
    <>
      <div className={styles.filters}>
        {months.map((month) => (
          <button
            key={month}
            className={selectedMonths.includes(month) ? styles.active : styles.filterButton}
            aria-pressed={selectedMonths.includes(month)}
            onClick={() => toggleMonth(month)}
          >
            {formatMonth(month)}
          </button>
        ))}
        {selectedMonths.length > 0 && (
          <button className={styles.filterButton} onClick={() => setSelectedMonths([])}>
            Show all
          </button>
        )}
      </div>

      <div className={styles.grid}>
        {visible.map((picture) => (
          <Link key={picture.date} to={`/apod/${picture.date}`}>
            <img className={styles.image} src={getImageUrl(picture)} alt={picture.title} />
          </Link>
        ))}
      </div>
    </>
  );

What's new here:

A fragment <> wraps the filter bar and the grid, since return needs a single element.

className switches with a ternary: selected buttons get the active style, and the rest get the normal style.

aria-pressed tells screen readers the button is a toggle and whether it's currently on. It's a small accessibility detail that counts toward design.

onClick={() => toggleMonth(month)} is the same lesson as onChange earlier: pass a function to run later, not a call that runs now.

"Show all" appears only when something is selected, using the && pattern.

The grid now maps over visible instead of withImages.

Section 5: Styles (add to GalleryView.module.css)

.filters {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 8px;
  margin-bottom: 16px;
}

.filterButton,
.active {
  padding: 6px 14px;
  border-radius: 999px;
  border: 1px solid #888;
  background: transparent;
  color: inherit;
  cursor: pointer;
}

.active {
  background: #3b5bdb;
  border-color: #3b5bdb;
  color: white;
}

display: flex with flex-wrap: wrap lays the buttons out in a row that wraps onto new lines on narrow screens.

border-radius: 999px gives the buttons pill-shaped ends.

.active repeats the base button styles (they're listed together at the top) and then overrides the colors, so a selected button is visibly filled in.

Test

You see about 3 month buttons, labeled like "September 2026."

Clicking one shows only that month's pictures, and the button fills in.

Clicking a second month shows both months together.

Clicking a selected month again deselects it.

"Show all" clears the selection and brings everything back.

When those pass, commit and push, and tell me. That completes the Gallery section of the rubric. Next is the Detail view, with its Previous and Next buttons.

