Wiring up navigation between consecutive pictures.

Done

The Gallery is done too. That's two of the three views complete.

Step 9: Detail view

This page needs to:

Read the date from the URL (/apod/2026-09-15).

Find that picture in the data.

Show its details.

Offer Previous and Next buttons that move through the pictures.

Section 1: Pass the data in (App.tsx)

Change the detail route to:

<Route path="/apod/:date" element={<DetailView pictures={pictures} loading={loading} />} />

The detail page also gets loading, because of direct URLs. If someone opens /mp2/apod/2026-09-15 in a fresh tab, the page renders before NASA has responded, while pictures is still empty. Without loading, the page would say "not found" for a moment, even though the picture is on its way.

Section 2: Imports and props (top of DetailView.tsx)

Replace the placeholder contents of the file with:

import { Link, useParams } from 'react-router';
import type { ApodPicture } from '../types';
import { getImageUrl } from '../utils';
import styles from './DetailView.module.css';

interface DetailViewProps {
  pictures: ApodPicture[];
  loading: boolean;
}

useParams is a React Router hook that reads the URL parameters defined in your route, the :date part.

Section 3: Find the picture and its neighbors (Zone 2)

export default function DetailView({ pictures, loading }: DetailViewProps) {
  const { date } = useParams();

  if (loading) return null;

  const index = pictures.findIndex((picture) => picture.date === date);

  if (index === -1) {
    return (
      <p>
        No picture found for {date}. <Link to="/">Back to the list</Link>
      </p>
    );
  }

  const picture = pictures[index];
  const prev = pictures[(index - 1 + pictures.length) % pictures.length];
  const next = pictures[(index + 1) % pictures.length];
  const imageUrl = getImageUrl(picture);

Here's each piece:

const { date } = useParams(); pulls date out of the URL. For /apod/2026-09-15, date is "2026-09-15".

if (loading) return null; renders nothing while data loads. App already shows "Loading...", so this page doesn't need its own message.

findIndex returns the position of the first item where your function returns true, or -1 if there's no match. You need the position, not just the picture, because Previous and Next are "one position back" and "one position forward."

The not-found case handles a date that isn't in the data, like a typo in the URL or a date older than 60 days. Showing a helpful message instead of crashing counts toward the assignment's error-handling expectations.

prev and next use % (modulo), which gives the remainder after division. It makes the buttons wrap around:

On the last picture, index + 1 equals the list length, and length % length is 0, so Next goes back to the first picture.

On the first picture, index - 1 would be -1. Adding pictures.length first keeps the number positive, so Previous goes to the last picture.

That way the buttons always work, and you never have to disable them at the ends.

The API returns pictures oldest first, so Previous means the earlier day and Next means the later day.

Section 4: The page content (Zone 3)

return (
    <article className={styles.detail}>
      <nav className={styles.pager}>
        <Link to={`/apod/${prev.date}`}>← Previous</Link>
        <Link to={`/apod/${next.date}`}>Next →</Link>
      </nav>

      <h1>{picture.title}</h1>
      <p className={styles.meta}>
        {picture.date}
        {picture.copyright && ` · © ${picture.copyright}`}
      </p>

      {imageUrl && <img className={styles.image} src={imageUrl} alt={picture.title} />}

      {picture.media_type === 'video' && (
        <p>
          <a href={picture.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
            Watch the video
          </a>
        </p>
      )}

      <p className={styles.explanation}>{picture.explanation}</p>
    </article>
  );
}

What's new here:

<article> is a semantic HTML tag for self-contained content, like one picture's page. It helps screen readers and search engines.

The copyright line only appears when copyright exists, using the && pattern. Many APOD images are public domain and have no copyright field, which is why you made it optional in your type.

For videos, the thumbnail shows (via getImageUrl), plus a link to watch the video.

Why <a> here and not Link? Link is for pages inside your app. The video lives on an outside site like YouTube, so a regular <a> is correct.
target="_blank" opens it in a new tab.

rel="noreferrer" is a security measure: it stops the new tab from getting access to your page.

Section 5: Styles (create src/pages/DetailView.module.css)

Remember to create this file in src/pages/, next to DetailView.tsx:

.detail {
  max-width: 800px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

.pager {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  margin-bottom: 16px;
}

.meta {
  color: #888;
}

.image {
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
  border-radius: 8px;
}

.explanation {
  line-height: 1.6;
}

max-width: 800px with margin: 0 auto keeps the page a readable width and centers it. Long lines of text are hard to read on wide screens.

justify-content: space-between pushes Previous to the left edge and Next to the right edge.

line-height: 1.6 adds breathing room between lines of the long explanation text.

Test

Clicking a row in the List, or an image in the Gallery, opens that picture's detail page.

The page shows the title, date, image, and explanation.

Next moves to the following day, and Previous to the day before.

On the newest picture, Next wraps to the oldest one.

A made-up date like /mp2/apod/1999-01-01 shows the "No picture found" message.

When those pass, commit and push. Then do one more test on your live site: open a detail page there, and refresh the browser. If you get a GitHub 404 page, that's a known GitHub Pages quirk, not a bug in your code, and fixing it is our next step. Tell me what happens either way.

