Defining theme variables and styling the header navigation.

Defining theme variables and styling the header navigation.

Done

Now direct URLs work on the live site too, so every functional requirement in the rubric is covered. What's left is design (10 points), then submission.

Step 11: Design

The goal is a consistent dark "space" theme. We'll set up global foundations first, then style each part.

Section 1: Normalize.css (recommended by the assignment)

Every browser has slightly different default styles for margins, form controls, headings, and so on. Normalize.css evens those out so your app looks the same everywhere. In the second terminal:

npm install normalize.css

Then in main.tsx, import it above ./index.css:

import 'normalize.css';
import './index.css';

Order matters in CSS: styles loaded later override earlier ones. Normalize sets the baseline, and your own styles build on top of it.

Section 2: The global theme (replace everything in src/index.css)

:root {
  --bg: #0b0d17;
  --surface: #151a2e;
  --text: #e6e8f0;
  --muted: #9aa3b8;
  --accent: #6c8cff;
  --border: #2a3150;
  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif;
  color-scheme: dark;
}

body {
  margin: 0;
  background: var(--bg);
  color: var(--text);
  line-height: 1.5;
}

a {
  color: var(--accent);
}

h1 {
  font-size: 1.8rem;
  margin: 0 0 8px;
}

This introduces CSS variables (also called custom properties):

Anything starting with -- defined on :root becomes a variable available to every CSS file, including your modules.

You use one with var(--accent).

The payoff: your whole color scheme lives in one place. If you want a different accent color, change one line instead of hunting through five files.

color-scheme: dark tells the browser the page is dark, so built-in parts like scrollbars and dropdown menus render dark too.

This file stays a normal global stylesheet, not a module, because these styles are meant to apply everywhere.

Section 3: Header and layout (App.tsx)

First, delete App.css and its import line, and create src/App.module.css (Section 4) instead. Then update the imports:

import { Routes, Route, Link, NavLink } from 'react-router';
import styles from './App.module.css';

Replace your return with:

return (
    <>
      <header className={styles.header}>
        <Link to="/" className={styles.brand}>
          Astronomy Picture of the Day
        </Link>
        <nav>
          <ul className={styles.nav}>
            <li>
              <NavLink to="/" end className={({ isActive }) => (isActive ? styles.activeLink : styles.link)}>
                List
              </NavLink>
            </li>
            <li>
              <NavLink to="/gallery" className={({ isActive }) => (isActive ? styles.activeLink : styles.link)}>
                Gallery
              </NavLink>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </nav>
      </header>

      <main className={styles.main}>
        {loading && <p className={styles.status}>Loading...</p>}
        {error && <p className={styles.error}>{error}</p>}

        <Routes>
          <Route path="/" element={<ListView pictures={pictures} />} />
          <Route path="/gallery" element={<GalleryView pictures={pictures} />} />
          <Route path="/apod/:date" element={<DetailView pictures={pictures} loading={loading} />} />
        </Routes>
      </main>
    </>
  );

What's new:

<header> and <main> are semantic tags, like <article> earlier. <main> marks the primary content, and screen readers use it to skip straight past the navigation.

NavLink is a Link that knows whether it points to the current page. Its className can be a function that receives { isActive } and returns which class to use. That's how the current tab gets highlighted.

end on the List link: without it, / would count as active on every page, since every path starts with /. end means "only active when the URL is exactly this."

Section 4: Header and layout styles (create src/App.module.css)

.header {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 12px;
  padding: 16px 24px;
  background: var(--surface);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
}

.brand {
  font-size: 1.2rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--text);
  text-decoration: none;
}

.nav {
  display: flex;
  gap: 8px;
  list-style: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}

.link,
.activeLink {
  padding: 6px 14px;
  border-radius: 6px;
  text-decoration: none;
  color: var(--muted);
}

.link:hover {
  color: var(--text);
}

.activeLink {
  background: var(--accent);
  color: white;
}

.main {
  max-width: 1100px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 24px 16px;
}

.status {
  color: var(--muted);
}

.error {
  color: #ff8080;
}

list-style: none removes the bullet points from the nav's <ul>. You keep the correct list structure for accessibility without the visual bullets. :hover styles an element while the mouse is over it.

Section 5: The list view

Create src/pages/ListView.module.css:

.controls {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 16px;
  align-items: flex-end;
  margin-bottom: 20px;
}

.control {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 4px;
  font-size: 0.9rem;
  color: var(--muted);
}

.search {
  flex: 1 1 240px;
}

.control input,
.control select {
  padding: 8px 10px;
  border-radius: 6px;
  border: 1px solid var(--border);
  background: var(--surface);
  color: var(--text);
  font-size: 1rem;
}

.list {
  list-style: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}

.row {
  display: flex;
  gap: 16px;
  padding: 12px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  color: var(--text);
  text-decoration: none;
}

.row:hover {
  background: var(--surface);
}

.date {
  min-width: 6.5rem;
  color: var(--muted);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}

.empty {
  color: var(--muted);
}

A few notes:

.control input is a descendant selector: it styles any <input> inside an element with the control class. This works in CSS Modules too.

flex: 1 1 240px on the search box means "start at 240px, then grow to fill spare space." The search box stretches while the dropdowns stay compact.

tabular-nums makes every digit the same width, so the dates line up in a neat column.

Then update ListView.tsx. Import the styles:

import styles from './ListView.module.css';

And replace the return with:

return (
    <>
      <div className={styles.controls}>
        <label className={`${styles.control} ${styles.search}`}>
          Search
          <input type="text" value={query} onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)} placeholder="e.g. galaxy" />
        </label>

        <label className={styles.control}>
          Sort by
          <select value={sortField} onChange={(e) => setSortField(e.target.value as 'title' | 'date')}>
            <option value="title">Title</option>
            <option value="date">Date</option>
          </select>
        </label>

        <label className={styles.control}>
          Order
          <select value={sortOrder} onChange={(e) => setSortOrder(e.target.value as 'asc' | 'desc')}>
            <option value="asc">Ascending</option>
            <option value="desc">Descending</option>
          </select>
        </label>
      </div>

      {sorted.length === 0 && <p className={styles.empty}>No pictures match "{query}".</p>}

      <ul className={styles.list}>
        {sorted.map((picture) => (
          <li key={picture.date}>
            <Link to={`/apod/${picture.date}`} className={styles.row}>
              <span className={styles.date}>{picture.date}</span>
              <span>{picture.title}</span>
            </Link>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </>
  );

What's new here:

Two classes on one element: the template literal `${styles.control} ${styles.search}` joins them with a space, just like class="a b" in HTML.

An empty-results message: if the search matches nothing, the user sees why the list is empty, rather than a blank page.

The whole row is the link, so the entire row is clickable, not just the text. The <span>s let the date and title be styled separately.

placeholder shows gray hint text in an empty search box.

Section 6: Use the theme variables in your other modules

Your Gallery and Detail styles have a few hard-coded colors. Swap them for the variables so everything matches:

In GalleryView.module.css:
border: 1px solid #888; becomes border: 1px solid var(--border);

In .active, #3b5bdb becomes var(--accent) (in both places).

In DetailView.module.css:
color: #888; becomes color: var(--muted);

Section 7: The browser tab title

In index.html, change the <title> to something like:

<title>APOD Explorer</title>

That's the text shown on the browser tab and in bookmarks. It currently says something like "Vite + React + TS."

Test

Click through every page and check:

The dark theme applies everywhere, with no white flashes or leftover default styling.

The nav highlights List or Gallery depending on the current page. (Neither highlights on a detail page, which is correct.)

The search box stretches, and list rows highlight on hover.

Searching for gibberish shows the "No pictures match" message.

Narrow the browser window to about phone width. The header, controls, and gallery should all wrap and stay usable, with no sideways scrolling.

Commit and push, then check the live site. When you're happy with it, tell me. What remains is submission: restoring the README with your sources, recording the demo video, and handling the LLM chat-log requirement.

