Embedding video content for astronomy picture data.

Embedding video content for astronomy picture data.

Done

Yes. APOD video URLs are usually YouTube or Vimeo embed links (like https://www.youtube.com/embed/abc123), made specifically for putting a video inside another page. Occasionally the URL is a direct video file (ending in .mp4), so we'll handle both cases.

An embed link goes in an <iframe>, a box that displays another web page inside yours. The YouTube player is its own small web page.

A direct video file goes in a <video> element, the browser's built-in player.

Section 1: A helper to tell them apart (add to src/utils.ts)

export function isVideoFile(url: string): boolean {
  return /\.(mp4|webm|ogg)$/i.test(url);
}

That's a regular expression, a pattern for matching text. Piece by piece:

\. matches a literal dot.

(mp4|webm|ogg) matches any one of those three extensions.

$ means "at the very end of the string."

i makes it case-insensitive, so .MP4 matches too.

So it returns true for ".../clip.mp4" and false for a YouTube embed link.

Section 2: Choose the media in Zone 2 (DetailView.tsx)

Add isVideoFile to your utils import:

import { getImageUrl, isVideoFile } from '../utils';

Then, right after the const imageUrl = getImageUrl(picture); line, add:

let media;
  if (picture.media_type === 'image') {
    media = <img className={styles.media} src={picture.url} alt={picture.title} />;
  } else if (isVideoFile(picture.url)) {
    media = <video className={styles.media} src={picture.url} controls />;
  } else {
    media = (
      <iframe
        className={styles.media}
        src={picture.url}
        title={picture.title}
        allow="fullscreen; picture-in-picture"
        allowFullScreen
      />
    );
  }

The new idea here is that JSX is just a value, so you can store it in a variable like anything else. Choosing between three options with a nested ternary inside the return would be hard to read. A plain if / else if / else in Zone 2 is much clearer, and then the JSX just drops in {media}.

The attributes:

controls on <video> shows the play, pause, and volume buttons.

title on the iframe is required for accessibility. Screen readers announce it so users know what the embedded frame contains.

allow and allowFullScreen let the embedded player go full screen and use picture-in-picture.

Section 3: Use it in Zone 3

In your return, replace these two blocks:

{imageUrl && <img className={styles.image} src={imageUrl} alt={picture.title} />}

{picture.media_type === 'video' && (
  <p>
    <a href={picture.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
      Watch the video
    </a>
  </p>
)}

with:

{media}

{picture.media_type === 'video' && (
  <p className={styles.meta}>
    Video not playing?{' '}
    <a href={picture.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
      Open it in a new tab
    </a>
  </p>
)}

The link stays as a fallback, because some video hosts block embedding on other sites, and the user shouldn't be stuck when that happens. The {' '} inserts a space: JSX drops spaces at the ends of lines, so without it you'd get "playing?Open."

You can also delete the const imageUrl = getImageUrl(picture); line, since media now handles every case. If you keep it, your linter will warn that it's unused.

Section 4: Styles (DetailView.module.css)

Rename .image to .media, and give videos a proper shape:

.media {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 8px;
}

iframe.media {
  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
}

border: 0 removes the default border browsers draw around iframes.

iframe.media targets only iframes with the media class. An iframe has no natural size, unlike an image, so without an aspect ratio it would render as a short, squashed box. 16 / 9 is the standard widescreen video shape.

Test

Find a video day. In the List, search won't help, but in the Gallery, video entries are the ones showing a YouTube-style thumbnail. Open its detail page, and the video should play right there.

Image days should look the same as before.

The "Open it in a new tab" link appears only on video days.

If none of your fetched dates happen to be videos, you can check by temporarily visiting a detail page for a date you know is a video. Or just tell me and we'll confirm the code another way. Then commit, push, and finish the Step 11 tests.

