Designing thumbnail fallbacks for missing video previews.

Designing thumbnail fallbacks for missing video previews.

Done

Most likely the videos aren't missing thumbnails so much as missing from the gallery entirely.

Why it happens

NASA only sends thumbnail_url for some videos, mainly YouTube ones. Many recent APOD videos are .mp4 files hosted on NASA's own site, and for those NASA provides no thumbnail at all.

Remember this line in GalleryView:

const withImages = pictures.filter((picture) => getImageUrl(picture) !== undefined);

For a video without a thumbnail_url, getImageUrl returns undefined, so that filter quietly removes it. The videos never reach the grid.

To confirm: open the Network tab, click the apod?api_key=... request, and open its Response (or Preview) tab. Find an entry with "media_type": "video". You'll likely see a url ending in .mp4 and no thumbnail_url field.

The fix: give every video a tile

Instead of dropping videos, we'll show the best thing available for each one:

Images use the picture itself.

Videos with a thumbnail use the thumbnail. For YouTube, we can also build one ourselves if NASA didn't send it.

.mp4 files use a paused <video> element, which displays the video's first frame.

Anything else gets a placeholder tile with the title.

Section 1: Smarter image lookup (src/utils.ts)

Replace getImageUrl with these two functions:

export function getYouTubeThumbnail(url: string): string | undefined {
  const match = url.match(/youtube\.com\/embed\/([\w-]+)/);
  return match ? `https://img.youtube.com/vi/${match[1]}/hqdefault.jpg` : undefined;
}

export function getImageUrl(picture: ApodPicture): string | undefined {
  if (picture.media_type === 'image') return picture.url;
  return picture.thumbnail_url ?? getYouTubeThumbnail(picture.url);
}

What's new:

YouTube thumbnails follow a predictable pattern: img.youtube.com/vi/<video ID>/hqdefault.jpg. The regular expression pulls the video ID out of the embed URL.
([\w-]+) means "capture one or more letters, digits, underscores, or dashes." The parentheses make it a capture group, and the captured text lands in match[1].

If the URL isn't a YouTube embed, match is null, and the function returns undefined.

?? (nullish coalescing) means "use the left side, unless it's null or undefined, in which case use the right side." So: NASA's thumbnail if it sent one, otherwise try building a YouTube one.

Section 2: A tile function (GalleryView.tsx)

Add isVideoFile to your utils import, and add ApodPicture if it isn't already imported:

import { getImageUrl, formatMonth, isVideoFile } from '../utils';

Then add this function above export default function GalleryView, outside the component:

function renderTile(picture: ApodPicture) {
  const imageUrl = getImageUrl(picture);

  if (imageUrl) {
    return <img className={styles.image} src={imageUrl} alt={picture.title} />;
  }

  if (isVideoFile(picture.url)) {
    return (
      <video
        className={styles.image}
        src={`${picture.url}#t=0.1`}
        muted
        preload="metadata"
        aria-label={picture.title}
      />
    );
  }

  return <div className={`${styles.image} ${styles.placeholder}`}>▶ {picture.title}</div>;
}

It lives outside the component because it doesn't use any state. It just turns a picture into a tile. Keeping it separate keeps the component's return short.

The video attributes:

preload="metadata" loads only the start of the video, enough to show a frame, not the whole file.

#t=0.1 on the end of the URL is a media fragment. It tells the browser to position the video at 0.1 seconds. Without it, some browsers (notably Safari) show a blank box, because they don't paint a frame until playback has started.

muted is a safe default for a preview, and it's required by some browsers before they'll load video automatically.

aria-label plays the role of an image's alt text, since <video> has no alt attribute.

Section 3: Use it in the component

Delete the withImages line. Every picture now gets a tile, so nothing needs to be filtered out.

Change the two places that used withImages to use pictures instead: the months line and the visible line.

In the grid, replace the <img ... /> inside each Link with:

{renderTile(picture)}

Section 4: Placeholder style (add to GalleryView.module.css)

.placeholder {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  padding: 12px;
  text-align: center;
  background: var(--surface);
  color: var(--muted);
  font-size: 0.9rem;
}

The placeholder also gets .image, which gives it the square shape, so it lines up with the other tiles. .placeholder adds the centered text and background.

Test

Video entries now appear in the gallery, showing a frame from the video or a YouTube thumbnail.

If any tile shows the ▶ placeholder, that entry had nothing previewable, which is the expected fallback.

Clicking a video tile opens its detail page, and the video plays there (from the previous step).

The month filter still works, and each month's count now includes its videos.

If videos still don't appear, paste one video entry's JSON from the Network tab (just the fields, not the explanation), and we'll look at exactly what NASA sent.

