Writing a shared toggle helper and styling the filter groups.

Writing a shared toggle helper and styling the filter groups.

Done

Good idea. It also shows off the "one or many attributes" requirement better, since users can combine filters.

How the two filters combine

With two filter groups, you need a rule for how they interact. The standard one, used by most shopping sites, is:

Within a group, OR: selecting August and September shows pictures from August or September.

Between groups, AND: selecting Videos plus September shows pictures that are videos and from September.

An empty group doesn't filter at all: no type selected means "any type."

Section 1: A reusable toggle helper (above the component in GalleryView.tsx)

You're about to need the same add-or-remove logic for a second list. Rather than writing toggleMonth again as toggleType, write it once, generically. Put this next to renderTile, outside the component:

function toggle<T>(list: T[], value: T): T[] {
  return list.includes(value) ? list.filter((v) => v !== value) : [...list, value];
}

The <T> makes this a generic function. T is a stand-in for "whatever type you call it with." Call it with a list of month strings, and T is string. Call it with a list of media types, and T is 'image' | 'video'. One function works for both, and TypeScript still checks that the value matches the list's type.

The logic is exactly your old toggleMonth, compressed into a ternary: if the value is in the list, return a copy without it; otherwise, return a copy with it added. It never modifies the original list, so React sees a new array and re-renders.

Section 2: The logic (Zone 2)

Replace your selectedMonths state line, the toggleMonth function, and the visible calculation with:

const [selectedMonths, setSelectedMonths] = useState<string[]>([]);
  const [selectedTypes, setSelectedTypes] = useState<('image' | 'video')[]>([]);

  const visible = pictures.filter((picture) => {
    const monthMatches =
      selectedMonths.length === 0 || selectedMonths.includes(picture.date.slice(0, 7));
    const typeMatches =
      selectedTypes.length === 0 || selectedTypes.includes(picture.media_type);
    return monthMatches && typeMatches;
  });

  const hasFilters = selectedMonths.length > 0 || selectedTypes.length > 0;

Keep your months line as it is.

What's happening here:

('image' | 'video')[] means "an array of media types." The parentheses matter. Without them, 'image' | 'video'[] would mean "either the string 'image', or an array of 'video's."

Each ...Matches line encodes the rules above. length === 0 || means "if nothing is selected in this group, it's a match." The OR within a group comes from .includes(), which checks against all selected values. The final && makes both groups have to pass.

hasFilters is computed once and used twice in the JSX below: to show the "Clear filters" button, and in the empty-results message.

Section 3: The filter bar (Zone 3)

Replace the whole <div className={styles.filters}>...</div> block with:

<div className={styles.filters}>
        <div className={styles.filterGroup}>
          <span className={styles.groupLabel}>Type</span>
          {(['image', 'video'] as const).map((type) => (
            <button
              key={type}
              className={selectedTypes.includes(type) ? styles.active : styles.filterButton}
              aria-pressed={selectedTypes.includes(type)}
              onClick={() => setSelectedTypes(toggle(selectedTypes, type))}
            >
              {type === 'image' ? 'Images' : 'Videos'}
            </button>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div className={styles.filterGroup}>
          <span className={styles.groupLabel}>Month</span>
          {months.map((month) => (
            <button
              key={month}
              className={selectedMonths.includes(month) ? styles.active : styles.filterButton}
              aria-pressed={selectedMonths.includes(month)}
              onClick={() => setSelectedMonths(toggle(selectedMonths, month))}
            >
              {formatMonth(month)}
            </button>
          ))}
        </div>

        {hasFilters && (
          <button
            className={styles.clearButton}
            onClick={() => {
              setSelectedMonths([]);
              setSelectedTypes([]);
            }}
          >
            Clear filters
          </button>
        )}
      </div>

      {visible.length === 0 && <p className={styles.empty}>No pictures match these filters.</p>}

Leave the grid <div> below it unchanged.

What's new:

['image', 'video'] as const fixes a typing problem. Normally TypeScript sees ['image', 'video'] as a plain string[], and setSelectedTypes would refuse a plain string. as const tells TypeScript "these exact values, and nothing else," so each type is typed as 'image' | 'video'. Mapping over a small array also means both buttons are written once, not copy-pasted.

The month buttons now use the generic toggle instead of toggleMonth.

The Clear button's onClick runs two statements, so the arrow function uses curly braces { } for a full function body instead of a single expression.

The empty message covers combinations with no results, like Videos plus a month that had no videos.

Section 4: Styles (GalleryView.module.css)

Replace your existing .filters rule with these:

.filters {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 12px;
  margin-bottom: 16px;
}

.filterGroup {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 8px;
}

.groupLabel {
  min-width: 3.5rem;
  color: var(--muted);
  font-size: 0.9rem;
}

.clearButton {
  padding: 0;
  border: 0;
  background: none;
  color: var(--accent);
  text-decoration: underline;
  cursor: pointer;
}

.empty {
  color: var(--muted);
}

flex-direction: column stacks the Type row, the Month row, and the Clear button vertically. Each row still wraps its own buttons horizontally.

align-items: flex-start keeps the Clear button at its natural width instead of stretching across the page.

min-width on the labels makes "Type" and "Month" take the same width, so both rows of buttons start at the same horizontal position.

The Clear button is styled as a link, which visually separates "reset everything" from the filter toggles themselves.

Test

Videos alone shows only video tiles.

Images alone shows only images.

Both types selected shows everything, same as none.

Videos plus one month shows only that month's videos. If it had none, you see the "No pictures match" message.

Clear filters resets both rows at once, and disappears when nothing is selected.

Commit and push when it passes. Then we'll wrap up with the submission steps.

