Clarifying that list view offers filtering rather than sorting.

Clarifying that list view offers filtering rather than sorting.

Done

Good call. Just to be precise about terms: this is filtering (hiding some items), not sorting (changing the order). The list will have both: sort by title or date, and filter by search text and type.

For the list, a dropdown fits better than toggle buttons, since it matches the Sort by and Order controls already there. We'll offer "All," "Images," and "Videos."

Section 1: New state (Zone 2 of ListView.tsx)

Add this below your other three state lines:

const [typeFilter, setTypeFilter] = useState<'all' | 'image' | 'video'>('all');

Unlike the gallery's array of selected types, this is a single value. A dropdown picks exactly one option, and 'all' stands for "don't filter by type."

Section 2: Update filtered

Replace your filtered line with:

const filtered = pictures.filter((picture) => {
  const matchesSearch = picture.title.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase());
  const matchesType = typeFilter === 'all' || picture.media_type === typeFilter;
  return matchesSearch && matchesType;
});

This is the same pattern as the gallery: one check per filter, combined with &&. The function body now has multiple lines, so the arrow function uses { } and an explicit return.

Your sorted line doesn't change, because it already works on whatever filtered contains. That's the benefit of computing the list in steps.

Section 3: The dropdown (Zone 3)

Add this inside <div className={styles.controls}>, right after the search label:

<label className={styles.control}>
          Type
          <select value={typeFilter} onChange={(e) => setTypeFilter(e.target.value as 'all' | 'image' | 'video')}>
            <option value="all">All</option>
            <option value="image">Images</option>
            <option value="video">Videos</option>
          </select>
        </label>

This is the same pattern as your sort dropdowns, including the as type assertion.

Section 4: Show which rows are videos

Once users can filter by type, they should be able to see each row's type too. Otherwise, "All" gives no hint which entries are videos. In the list's Link, add a badge after the title:

<Link to={`/apod/${picture.date}`} className={styles.row}>
  <span className={styles.date}>{picture.date}</span>
  <span>{picture.title}</span>
  {picture.media_type === 'video' && <span className={styles.badge}>Video</span>}
</Link>

Add this to ListView.module.css:

.badge {
  margin-left: auto;
  padding: 2px 8px;
  border-radius: 999px;
  border: 1px solid var(--accent);
  color: var(--accent);
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  align-self: center;
}

margin-left: auto pushes the badge to the far right of the row. In a flex row, an auto margin absorbs all the leftover space on that side.

align-self: center vertically centers the small badge against the taller title text.

Section 5: Update the empty message

The old message, No pictures match "{query}", would be misleading if the type filter caused the empty result. Change it to:

{sorted.length === 0 && <p className={styles.empty}>No pictures match your search and filters.</p>}

Test

Videos shows only rows with the Video badge.

Images shows only rows without it.

Videos plus a search narrows by both at once.

Sorting still works on the filtered results.

All brings everything back.

Commit and push when it works. Then we'll do the submission steps: README and sources, the demo video, and the LLM chat-log requirement.

