Planning navigation state for picture browsing between pages.

Planning navigation state for picture browsing between pages.

Done

Right now DetailView always steps through all pictures in date order, because it only receives the full pictures array. It has no idea what list you clicked from, or how that list was filtered and sorted.

The fix is to send the list along with the click. React Router lets a Link carry extra data called state. It travels with the navigation, invisible in the URL. So when you click an item, the list or gallery will send the ordered list of dates it's currently showing. The detail page will use that sequence for Previous and Next, and pass it along on every step so it's never lost.

If there's no state (for example, someone pastes a detail URL directly), the detail page falls back to all pictures by date, the current behavior.

Section 1: A shared type (add to src/types.ts)

export interface DetailNavState {
  dates: string[];
}

Three files will send or read this data, so describing its shape once keeps them in agreement. We send only the dates, not full picture objects: the dates are enough to identify each picture, and they're much smaller.

Section 2: Send the sequence from the list (ListView.tsx)

In Zone 2, after your sorted line, add:

const navState: DetailNavState = { dates: sorted.map((picture) => picture.date) };

Add DetailNavState to your types import:

import type { ApodPicture, DetailNavState } from '../types';

Then add state={navState} to the row's Link:

<Link to={`/apod/${picture.date}`} state={navState} className={styles.row}>

sorted is exactly what's on screen: searched, type-filtered, and in the chosen order. So the detail page receives precisely that sequence.

Section 3: Send the sequence from the gallery (GalleryView.tsx)

This is the same idea. In Zone 2, after visible:

const navState: DetailNavState = { dates: visible.map((picture) => picture.date) };

Import the type the same way, and add state={navState} to the tile's Link:

<Link key={picture.date} to={`/apod/${picture.date}`} state={navState}>

Section 4: Use the sequence in DetailView.tsx

Update the imports:

import { Link, useParams, useLocation } from 'react-router';
import type { ApodPicture, DetailNavState } from '../types';

Then replace everything from const { date } = useParams(); down to the const next = ... line with:

const { date } = useParams();
  const location = useLocation();

  if (loading) return null;

  const picture = pictures.find((p) => p.date === date);

  if (!picture) {
    return (
      <p>
        No picture found for {date}. <Link to="/">Back to the list</Link>
      </p>
    );
  }

  const navState = location.state as DetailNavState | null;
  const sequence =
    navState?.dates && navState.dates.includes(picture.date)
      ? navState.dates
      : pictures.map((p) => p.date);

  const position = sequence.indexOf(picture.date);
  const prevDate = sequence[(position - 1 + sequence.length) % sequence.length];
  const nextDate = sequence[(position + 1) % sequence.length];
  const nextState: DetailNavState = { dates: sequence };

Here's what changed:

useLocation() gives you the current location, including any state sent with the navigation.

It's called before if (loading) return null;, and that placement is required. React has a rule that hooks (functions starting with use) must run in the same order on every render. If a hook came after an early return, it would run on some renders but not others, and React would crash. So: all hooks at the top, before any early return.

find instead of findIndex. The picture's position in the full list no longer matters. What matters is its position in the sequence.

as DetailNavState | null: the router types state loosely, as unknown, because any page could send anything. We assert what we expect, and include null for "nothing was sent."

navState?.dates: the ?. is optional chaining. It means "if navState is null or undefined, stop and give undefined, instead of crashing on .dates."

The fallback check, .includes(picture.date), guards against stale state. If the sequence doesn't contain the current picture, the state didn't come from this navigation, so we ignore it and use all pictures.

Previous and Next now work on dates, using the same wrap-around arithmetic as before, just on sequence instead of pictures.

nextState is the same sequence, ready to be passed forward on each step.

Section 5: Update the Previous and Next links, and show the position

In Zone 3, replace the <nav> block with:

<nav className={styles.pager}>
        <Link to={`/apod/${prevDate}`} state={nextState}>← Previous</Link>
        <span className={styles.meta}>
          {position + 1} of {sequence.length}
        </span>
        <Link to={`/apod/${nextDate}`} state={nextState}>Next →</Link>
      </nav>

state={nextState} on both links is what keeps the sequence alive. Without it, the first Next click would arrive with no state and fall back to all pictures.

"3 of 12" tells the user they're moving through a filtered set. Otherwise, it would look odd that Next skips days. position + 1 converts the zero-based position to the counting humans expect.

Your existing .pager style, with space-between, puts the counter in the middle automatically.

Test

In the list, filter to Videos, click one, and press Next repeatedly. You should only see videos, with a counter like "2 of 5," wrapping around at the end.

In the list, sort by Title ascending, click a row, and press Next. You should move alphabetically.

In the gallery, select one month, click a picture, and step through. You should stay within that month.

Paste a detail URL into a new tab. Next and Previous should step through all pictures by date, with a counter out of the full total.

Refresh in the middle of a filtered sequence. It should stay filtered, because the browser keeps navigation state in the tab's history across reloads.

Commit and push when it works. After that, the functionality is complete, and we'll move on to the submission steps.

