Extracting a shared thumbnail component for both views.

Extracting a shared thumbnail component for both views.

Done

We'll add the thumbnail by pulling the gallery's tile logic into a shared component, so the list and gallery both use it. The logic for "image, video thumbnail, video frame, or placeholder" is non-trivial, so it should exist in exactly one place. If you copied it into ListView, any future fix would have to be made twice, and the two copies would eventually drift apart.

Section 1: The shared component

Create a new folder, src/components/, and inside it Thumbnail.tsx:

import type { ApodPicture } from '../types';
import { getImageUrl, isVideoFile } from '../utils';
import styles from './Thumbnail.module.css';

interface ThumbnailProps {
  picture: ApodPicture;
  className?: string;
}

export default function Thumbnail({ picture, className = '' }: ThumbnailProps) {
  const imageUrl = getImageUrl(picture);
  const classes = `${styles.thumb} ${className}`;

  if (imageUrl) {
    return <img className={classes} src={imageUrl} alt={picture.title} loading="lazy" />;
  }

  if (isVideoFile(picture.url)) {
    return (
      <video
        className={classes}
        src={`${picture.url}#t=0.1`}
        muted
        preload="metadata"
        aria-label={picture.title}
      />
    );
  }

  return (
    <div className={`${classes} ${styles.placeholder}`} role="img" aria-label={picture.title}>
      ▶
    </div>
  );
}

What's new:

pages/ vs. components/: pages are what routes point to. Components are building blocks used inside pages. This folder split is a common convention, and it matches the file-structure guide the assignment links to.

className?: string with a default of '': the component doesn't decide its own size. The page using it passes a class. The gallery wants big squares, and the list wants small ones. The ? makes the prop optional, and = '' provides a default if it's left out.

Two classes combined: styles.thumb (the base look, from this component's own CSS) plus whatever the page passes in (size and shape).

loading="lazy" tells the browser not to download an image until it's about to scroll into view. This matters a lot here. APOD has no small version of its images, so each thumbnail is the full-size picture, sometimes several megabytes. Without lazy loading, opening the list would download around 180 large images at once. With it, only the visible ones load.

role="img" with aria-label on the placeholder <div> tells screen readers to treat it as an image with that name, since a <div> can't have alt text.

Section 2: The component's styles (create src/components/Thumbnail.module.css)

.thumb {
  display: block;
  object-fit: cover;
}

.placeholder {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  background: var(--surface);
  color: var(--muted);
}

Only the base look lives here: cropping to fill, and the placeholder's appearance. The size comes from the page that uses it.

Section 3: Use it in the gallery (GalleryView.tsx)

Delete the renderTile function.

Import the component, and remove getImageUrl and isVideoFile from the utils import if nothing else in the file uses them (keep formatMonth):
import Thumbnail from '../components/Thumbnail';

Inside each tile's Link, replace {renderTile(picture)} with:
<Thumbnail picture={picture} className={styles.image} />

In GalleryView.module.css, you can delete the .placeholder rule, since the component has its own now.

Your existing .image class already provides the gallery's square sizing.

Section 4: Use it in the list (ListView.tsx)

Import it:

import Thumbnail from '../components/Thumbnail';

Then add it as the first item in each row's Link:

<Link to={`/apod/${picture.date}`} state={navState} className={styles.row}>
  <Thumbnail picture={picture} className={styles.thumb} />
  <span className={styles.date}>{picture.date}</span>
  <span>{picture.title}</span>
  {picture.media_type === 'video' && <span className={styles.badge}>Video</span>}
</Link>

Section 5: List styles (ListView.module.css)

Add a .thumb rule, and add align-items: center to your existing .row rule:

.row {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 16px;
  padding: 12px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  color: var(--text);
  text-decoration: none;
}

.thumb {
  width: 56px;
  height: 56px;
  border-radius: 4px;
  flex-shrink: 0;
}

align-items: center vertically centers the text next to the thumbnail, which is now the tallest thing in the row.

flex-shrink: 0 stops the thumbnail from getting squeezed. By default, flex items shrink when space runs out, so on a narrow phone screen with a long title, the image would get crushed into a sliver. This keeps it at 56px while the title wraps instead.

Test

Each list row shows a small square image to the left of the date.

Video rows show a YouTube thumbnail, a video frame, or the ▶ placeholder.

The gallery looks the same as before (it now uses the shared component).

Narrow the window to phone width. The thumbnails keep their size while titles wrap.

