Designing the layout for list item rows.

Designing the layout for list item rows.

Done

Right now each row is a flex row that packs everything from the left edge, and nothing claims the extra space. A CSS grid row fixes that: you declare columns, and one of them takes up the leftover width.

The new layout for each row: thumbnail on the left, title in the middle (taking all the spare space), date aligned to the right. On narrow screens, the date will move under the title, so nothing gets squeezed.

Section 1: Restructure the row (ListView.tsx)

Replace the contents of each row's Link with:

<Link to={`/apod/${picture.date}`} state={navState} className={styles.row}>
  <Thumbnail picture={picture} className={styles.thumb} />
  <span className={styles.titleCell}>
    <span className={styles.title}>{picture.title}</span>
    {picture.media_type === 'video' && <span className={styles.badge}>Video</span>}
  </span>
  <span className={styles.date}>{picture.date}</span>
</Link>

Two changes:

The date moved to the end, so it lands in the right-hand column.

The title and badge share one wrapper, titleCell. A grid places its children into columns in order. The badge only exists on video rows, so if it were a direct child, video rows would have four children and image rows three, and the columns would misalign between rows. Wrapping them keeps every row at exactly three children: thumbnail, title cell, date.

Section 2: The row layout (ListView.module.css)

Replace your existing .row, .thumb, .date, and .badge rules with these, and add the new ones:

.row {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 72px 1fr auto;
  align-items: center;
  column-gap: 20px;
  padding: 14px 16px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  color: var(--text);
  text-decoration: none;
}

.row:hover {
  background: var(--surface);
}

.thumb {
  width: 72px;
  height: 72px;
  border-radius: 6px;
}

.titleCell {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 10px;
}

.title {
  font-size: 1.05rem;
  font-weight: 500;
}

.date {
  color: var(--muted);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  white-space: nowrap;
}

.badge {
  padding: 2px 8px;
  border-radius: 999px;
  border: 1px solid var(--accent);
  color: var(--accent);
  font-size: 0.75rem;
}

Here's how it works:

grid-template-columns: 72px 1fr auto defines three columns:
72px: a fixed width for the thumbnail.

1fr: "one fraction of the remaining space." It's the only fractional column, so it gets all the leftover width. That's what spreads the row out.

auto: just wide enough for its content, the date. Being last, it sits against the right edge.

column-gap: 20px puts consistent spacing between the columns.

The thumbnail is a bit bigger (72px), and the title slightly larger and bolder, so the title reads as the main thing in each row.

white-space: nowrap keeps the date on one line, so it never breaks into 2026-09- and 15.

The badge no longer needs margin-left: auto, since it now sits right next to the title inside the title cell. The thumbnail's flex-shrink is also gone, because grid columns with fixed widths don't shrink.

Section 3: Narrow screens (add at the bottom of ListView.module.css)

@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .row {
    grid-template-columns: 56px 1fr;
    column-gap: 12px;
  }

  .thumb {
    width: 56px;
    height: 56px;
  }

  .date {
    grid-column: 2;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
}

A media query applies its styles only when a condition is true. Here, that's when the window is 600px wide or less, roughly phone size. On a phone:

The grid drops to two columns: thumbnail and text.

grid-column: 2 places the date in the second column. Since the first row of that column is already taken by the title, the date wraps onto the next row, directly under the title.

The thumbnail shrinks back to 56px to leave room for text.

Test

On a wide window, each row shows the thumbnail on the left, the title filling the middle, and the date against the right edge.

Dates line up in a neat column down the right side.

Video rows show the badge right after the title, and the columns stay aligned with the image rows.

Narrow the window below 600px. The date should move under the title.

Commit and push when you're happy with it.

