Axios is used in exactly one file, src/api/apod.ts. It's the only place your app talks to NASA. Everything else just calls the function that file exports.

The code

import axios from 'axios';
import type { ApodPicture } from '../types';

const API_KEY = '...';
const BASE_URL = 'https://api.nasa.gov/planetary/apod';

export async function fetchPictures(startDate: string): Promise<ApodPicture[]> {
  const response = await axios.get<ApodPicture[]>(BASE_URL, {
    params: {
      api_key: API_KEY,
      start_date: startDate,
      thumbs: true,
    },
  });
  return response.data;
}

What Axios does here

axios.get(...) sends an HTTP GET request, the kind of request your browser makes when you open a URL. It's the same request you made by hand when you opened the APOD URL in your browser back at the start.

params builds the query string for you. Axios turns this object into:

https://api.nasa.gov/planetary/apod?api_key=...&start_date=2026-04-01&thumbs=true

It also URL-encodes the values, escaping special characters, so you never have to piece the string together by hand.

api_key: your NASA key.

start_date: how far back to fetch. The end date defaults to NASA's "today."

thumbs: true: asks NASA to include thumbnail URLs for video entries.

<ApodPicture[]> is the TypeScript part. It tells TypeScript the response data will be an array of ApodPicture, so response.data is fully typed everywhere it's used.

response.data holds the body of NASA's response. Axios parses the JSON into JavaScript objects automatically. The response object also has other fields, like status (200) and headers, but you only need the data.

async / await: the request takes time. await pauses this function until NASA answers, without freezing the page. The function returns a Promise<ApodPicture[]>: a list of pictures, eventually.

Where it's called: App.tsx

useEffect(() => {
  // ...compute startDate...
  fetchPictures(startDate)
    .then((data) => setPictures(data))
    .catch(() => setError('Could not load pictures from NASA. Please try again later.'))
    .finally(() => setLoading(false));
}, []);

It runs once, when the app first loads (useEffect with []).

Success: the pictures go into state and flow to every page as props.

Failure: a friendly message goes into state and shows on screen.

Either way: loading turns off.

So the whole app makes one Axios request per page load. Searching, sorting, filtering, and navigating to detail pages all use the data already in memory, with no further requests. That keeps the app fast and well inside NASA's rate limit, which the assignment specifically asks you to think about.

Why Axios instead of the browser's built-in fetch

The browser has a built-in fetch function that can do the same job. The assignment requires Axios, and it does save work:

Automatic JSON parsing. With fetch, you'd need a separate await response.json() step.

The params object. With fetch, you'd build the query string yourself.

Errors on bad responses. If NASA returns an error status like 403 (bad key), 429 (rate limit), or 500 (server problem), Axios rejects the promise, so your .catch runs and the user sees your error message. fetch treats those as successful responses, so you'd have to check response.ok yourself. Otherwise, an error could slip through as if it were data.

Typed responses through the axios.get<T> generic.

Why it's isolated in one file

Keeping all API code in api/apod.ts means:

Components don't know or care about URLs, keys, or HTTP. They just call fetchPictures.

If the API changes, or you needed mock data because NASA was down (the assignment suggests this), you'd only change this one file.

The API key lives in one place.

