Your code isn't the problem; NASA changed the API underneath you. The APOD site moved from apod.nasa.gov to science.nasa.gov/apod on September 29, and the old API URL your app calls is no longer usable. On October 1, other developers reported every request returning HTTP 500 or 503 errors after 11 to 26 seconds. When it does answer, it returns the NASA logo as the image, "NASA Science" as the title, and today's explanation for every date.

NASA's API page says the APOD API now returns information from https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic, and the legacy API will be archived on December 1, 2026. It also says the thumbs, hd, and concept_tags parameters are disabled in the new service.

The assignment anticipated this: "The API you are working with may become temporarily unavailable..." Handling it well is part of the work, and the good news is that we kept all API code in one file, so most of the app won't need to change.

What's different in the new API

I tested the new endpoint directly:

No API key needed.

The date parameters are ignored. I requested July dates and got the latest entries anyway. Another developer reported the same thing: it just returns the 25 most recent entries no matter what parameters you send. So you get about the last 25 days, no more.

The fields changed meaning:
url is now a link to the article page, not the image.

hdurl is the actual image.

explanation, title, and copyright now contain HTML (links, bold tags), not plain text.

There's a new alt field with a written description of the image, which is great for accessibility.

permalink links to the article page.

No thumbnails for videos (thumbs is disabled).

Step 17: Switch to the new API

The strategy is called an adapter. api/apod.ts will fetch the new format and convert each entry into the ApodPicture shape your components already use. The pages don't need to know NASA changed anything.

Section 1: Add two optional fields (types.ts)

Add these to your ApodPicture interface:

alt?: string;
  permalink?: string;

Section 2: Replace api/apod.ts entirely

import axios from 'axios';
import type { ApodPicture } from '../types';

const BASE_URL = 'https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic';

interface RawApodEntry {
  date: string;
  title: string;
  media_type: string;
  explanation: string;
  copyright?: string;
  alt?: string;
  url: string;
  hdurl?: string;
  permalink: string;
}

function htmlToText(html: string | undefined): string {
  if (!html) return '';
  const doc = new DOMParser().parseFromString(html, 'text/html');
  return (doc.body.textContent ?? '').trim();
}

function toPicture(raw: RawApodEntry): ApodPicture {
  const isVideo = raw.media_type === 'video';
  const mainText = raw.explanation.split('<br><br>')[0];

  return {
    date: raw.date,
    title: htmlToText(raw.title),
    explanation: htmlToText(mainText).replace(/^Explanation:\s*/, ''),
    media_type: isVideo ? 'video' : 'image',
    url: isVideo ? raw.url : (raw.hdurl ?? raw.url),
    hdurl: raw.hdurl,
    copyright: htmlToText(raw.copyright) || undefined,
    alt: raw.alt,
    permalink: raw.permalink,
  };
}

export async function fetchPictures(): Promise<ApodPicture[]> {
  const response = await axios.get<RawApodEntry[]>(BASE_URL);
  return response.data.map(toPicture);
}

Here's what each piece does:

RawApodEntry describes what NASA actually sends now. ApodPicture describes what your app wants. Having two types makes the conversion explicit, and TypeScript checks both sides.

htmlToText turns HTML into plain text. DOMParser is built into the browser: it reads an HTML string into a document, and textContent gives you just the words, with the tags removed. So <a href="...">Pawel Piechnik</a> becomes Pawel Piechnik. Parsing this way never runs anything inside the HTML; it only reads the text.

.split('<br><br>')[0]: NASA now appends site announcements to every explanation ("APOD's email for image submissions has changed..."), separated by a double line break. Taking only the part before the first <br><br> keeps just the real explanation.

.replace(/^Explanation:\s*/, '') removes the "Explanation:" label at the start. ^ means "at the very beginning," and \s* means "any spaces after it."

raw.hdurl ?? raw.url: for images, use the actual image file. The ?? falls back to url if hdurl is ever missing.

htmlToText(...) || undefined: if there's no copyright, this gives undefined instead of an empty string, so your {picture.copyright && ...} check in DetailView still hides the line.

media_type is normalized to exactly 'image' or 'video', so the rest of your app can keep trusting that union type.

.map(toPicture) converts every entry. This is the adapter in one line.

Section 3: Update the fetch in App.tsx

The date math no longer does anything, since the API ignores dates. Replace the inside of your useEffect with:

useEffect(() => {
    fetchPictures()
      .then((data) => setPictures(data))
      .catch(() => setError('Could not load pictures from NASA. Please try again later.'))
      .finally(() => setLoading(false));
  }, []);

Section 4: Use the new alt text (components/Thumbnail.tsx and DetailView.tsx)

In both files, wherever an <img> has alt={picture.title}, change it to:

alt={picture.alt ?? picture.title}

NASA's alt text actually describes what's in the picture ("A starfield has a bright pink swirling nebula..."), which is far more useful to a screen-reader user than repeating the title.

Section 5: Fix the video fallback link (DetailView.tsx)

In the "Video not playing?" link, change href={picture.url} to:

href={picture.permalink ?? picture.url}

None of the current entries are videos, so I couldn't check how the new API formats them. If a video shows up and won't play in the frame, this link now goes to NASA's article page, which will definitely work.

Test it

Save everything and check the List, Gallery, and Detail pages. Images should load again, and explanations should read as clean text with no HTML tags.

Open the browser console (Cmd + Option + J). If you see a red error mentioning CORS ("blocked by CORS policy"), the browser is refusing to let your page read from NASA's new server. Tell me if that happens. The assignment explicitly allows a fallback (mock data), and we'd set that up next.

Run npm run build to confirm no type errors, then commit and push.

