Step 18: Replace the Month filter with Day of the week

With only the latest 25 entries, the Month filter shows just one or two buttons. A Day-of-the-week filter gives seven buttons, each matching three or four pictures, so every click visibly changes the gallery. And now that two of the entries are videos, the Type filter visibly works too.

Section 1: Weekday helpers (utils.ts)

Add these, and delete formatMonth if nothing else uses it:

export const WEEKDAYS = ['Sunday', 'Monday', 'Tuesday', 'Wednesday', 'Thursday', 'Friday', 'Saturday'];

export function weekdayOf(date: string): string {
  return WEEKDAYS[new Date(`${date}T00:00:00`).getDay()];
}

.getDay() returns a number from 0 (Sunday) to 6 (Saturday). Using it as an index into WEEKDAYS turns it into a name. That's why the array starts with Sunday.

T00:00:00 is the same time-zone guard as before. Without it, "2026-09-27" would be read as UTC midnight, which is still the 26th in the US, so every picture would land on the wrong day.

Section 2: Imports (GalleryView.tsx)

Change the utils import to:

import { WEEKDAYS, weekdayOf } from '../utils';

Section 3: The logic (Zone 2)

Replace the selectedMonths state, the months line, and the visible and hasFilters calculations with:

const [selectedDays, setSelectedDays] = useState<string[]>([]);
  const [selectedTypes, setSelectedTypes] = useState<('image' | 'video')[]>([]);

  const days = WEEKDAYS.filter((day) => pictures.some((picture) => weekdayOf(picture.date) === day));

  const visible = pictures.filter((picture) => {
    const dayMatches = selectedDays.length === 0 || selectedDays.includes(weekdayOf(picture.date));
    const typeMatches = selectedTypes.length === 0 || selectedTypes.includes(picture.media_type);
    return dayMatches && typeMatches;
  });

  const hasFilters = selectedDays.length > 0 || selectedTypes.length > 0;

Keep your navState line as it is.

The days line keeps the buttons in calendar order. If you collected the weekdays from the data with a Set and sorted them, they'd come out alphabetically: Friday, Monday, Saturday... That's useless for days of the week. Instead, it starts from the already-ordered WEEKDAYS list and keeps only the days that appear in the data. .some() returns true if at least one picture falls on that day.

