Designing caching and on-demand fetching to limit requests.

Read a page

Designing caching and on-demand fetching to limit requests.

Done

Good detective work. I checked the link you found, and it works: /apod-basic/260704 returns the July 4th entry ("Pathfinder on Mars") as a single object, not an array, well outside the 25-day window.

The docs never mention this route, but there was a clue: every entry's basic_html_url field looks like .../apod-basic/260704/html. The number is the date in YYMMDD form (26 for 2026, 07 for July, 04 for the 4th). Drop the /html from the end and you get that day's full entry.

So now we can load more than 25 days:

One request to the list endpoint gives the latest 25 days.

Individual requests fill in the older days, one per date.

Step 19: Load more days

Section 1: Two new helpers in api/apod.ts

Add these above fetchPictures:

function toDateId(date: Date): string {
  const yy = String(date.getFullYear()).slice(2);
  const mm = String(date.getMonth() + 1).padStart(2, '0');
  const dd = String(date.getDate()).padStart(2, '0');
  return `${yy}${mm}${dd}`;
}

async function fetchOne(dateId: string): Promise<ApodPicture> {
  const response = await axios.get<RawApodEntry>(`${BASE_URL}/${dateId}`, {
    params: { api_key: API_KEY },
  });
  return toPicture(response.data);
}

toDateId turns a Date into NASA's ID format.
.slice(2) keeps the last two digits of the year: "2026" becomes "26".

getMonth() counts from 0 (January is 0), so you add 1.

.padStart(2, '0') adds a leading zero when needed: "7" becomes "07".

fetchOne requests a single day. Notice the type is RawApodEntry, not RawApodEntry[], because this route returns one object. It reuses your existing toPicture adapter, so single entries come out in exactly the same shape as the list.

Section 2: Replace fetchPictures

export async function fetchPictures(totalDays = 60): Promise<ApodPicture[]> {
  const response = await axios.get<RawApodEntry[]>(BASE_URL, {
    params: { api_key: API_KEY },
  });
  const latest = response.data.map(toPicture);

  const earliest = latest.reduce((min, p) => (p.date < min ? p.date : min), latest[0].date);
  const cursor = new Date(`${earliest}T00:00:00`);
  const olderIds: string[] = [];
  for (let i = latest.length; i < totalDays; i++) {
    cursor.setDate(cursor.getDate() - 1);
    olderIds.push(toDateId(cursor));
  }

  const results = await Promise.allSettled(olderIds.map(fetchOne));
  const older = results
    .filter((r): r is PromiseFulfilledResult<ApodPicture> => r.status === 'fulfilled')
    .map((r) => r.value);

  return [...latest, ...older].sort((a, b) => a.date.localeCompare(b.date));
}

Here's how it works:

totalDays = 60 is a default parameter. Calling fetchPictures() loads 60 days, and fetchPictures(90) would load 90. Your App.tsx call doesn't need to change.

reduce finds the oldest date in the latest batch. It walks through the array carrying a running result, here "the smallest date so far." String comparison works because of the YYYY-MM-DD format, the same trick your sorting relies on.

The loop steps backward one day at a time from that oldest date, collecting an ID for each day until the total reaches totalDays. With 25 from the list and a default of 60, that's 35 more days.

Promise.allSettled sends all 35 requests at once and waits for every one to finish, whether it succeeded or failed. Compare that with Promise.all, which fails entirely if even one request fails. If NASA happens to be missing a single day, you'd lose all the older pictures. With allSettled, one bad day is simply skipped.

The filter with r is PromiseFulfilledResult<ApodPicture> is a type predicate. Each result is either { status: 'fulfilled', value } or { status: 'rejected', reason }. The predicate tells TypeScript "everything that passes this filter is the fulfilled kind," so the next line can safely read r.value.

The final .sort puts everything in date order, oldest first.

That last sort also fixes a bug that came in quietly with the API migration. The old API returned pictures oldest first, but the new list endpoint returns them newest first. DetailView's fallback sequence (used for pasted URLs) is the raw pictures order, so Previous and Next have been running backward since the switch. Sorting here restores the order everything else assumes.

Section 3: Clean up older copyright text (in toPicture)

Older entries format their credit differently: the copyright field you saw for July 4th starts with a bold "Image Credit:" label. Strip it the same way you strip "Explanation:":

copyright: htmlToText(raw.copyright).replace(/^Image Credit:\s*/, '') || undefined,

Section 4: Cache the results (recommended)

Loading the page now makes 36 requests, and the assignment specifically suggests caching large, common API calls. sessionStorage is a small key-value store built into the browser. It keeps data for as long as the tab stays open, so refreshing the page or pasting a detail URL won't re-download everything.

Rename the fetchPictures you just wrote to fetchFromNasa (make it a plain async function, without export), then add this below it:

const CACHE_KEY = 'apod-pictures';

export async function fetchPictures(totalDays = 60): Promise<ApodPicture[]> {
  const cached = sessionStorage.getItem(CACHE_KEY);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached) as ApodPicture[];

  const pictures = await fetchFromNasa(totalDays);
  sessionStorage.setItem(CACHE_KEY, JSON.stringify(pictures));
  return pictures;
}

sessionStorage only stores strings, so JSON.stringify turns the array into text, and JSON.parse turns it back.

The cache stores the converted pictures, not NASA's raw responses. The basic_html field is large, and we've already pulled out what we need from it, so the cached data stays small.

Only a successful load is cached. If NASA fails, fetchFromNasa throws before setItem runs, so an error never gets saved.

To test with fresh data, open a new tab, or clear it in DevTools under Application → Session Storage.

Test

The List shows about 60 entries, reaching back into early August.

Sorting by Date ascending starts at the oldest date, and descending at today.

Previous and Next on a pasted URL step to the day before and the day after again.

Old entries, like early August ones, show clean credits without "Image Credit:."

Refresh the page. It should load almost instantly, from the cache. In the Network tab, you should see no new apod-basic requests.

About the filters

With 60 days, the data spans three months again, so you have a choice for the gallery:

Keep Day of the week. It works well with any amount of data.

Switch back to Month. It's more meaningful for dates, and your old code still works.

Either satisfies the rubric. Day of the week is the safer choice if you ever drop back to fewer days.

When it's working, run npm run lint and npm run build, commit, and push. Also update your README note: the app combines the list endpoint with the undocumented per-date route, /apod-basic/YYMMDD, which you discovered from the basic_html_url field. That's worth a sentence in your demo video too.

