Flagging concurrency, storage, and image-sizing concerns across the code.

Flagging concurrency, storage, and image-sizing concerns across the code.

Done

Mostly yes. App.tsx, types.ts, DetailView.tsx, and Thumbnail.tsx are all correct. One thing in api/apod.ts needs fixing, and a few more are worth tightening.

Must fix: the cache key

CACHE_KEY is still the fixed string 'apod-pictures'. This is the exact problem from your last question: if you change 150 to anything else, the app keeps serving whatever was cached before. Build the key from totalDays.

Should fix: two robustness gaps

1. An empty response would crash the app. This line assumes NASA returned at least one entry:

const earliest = latest.reduce((min, p) => (p.date < min ? p.date : min), latest[0].date);

If the list ever comes back empty, as it might during NASA's migration, latest[0] is undefined, and .date throws an error. Add a guard right after latest is created:

if (latest.length === 0) return [];

2. Storage can fail. sessionStorage.setItem can throw an error, for example in some private-browsing modes or if storage is full. Right now, that would throw away 150 pictures you had successfully fetched and show the error message instead. Saving to the cache is a bonus, so a failure there shouldn't break the app. Wrap the storage calls in try/catch.

Here are both fixes together. Replace everything from const CACHE_KEY to the end of the file with:

export async function fetchPictures(totalDays = 150): Promise<ApodPicture[]> {
  const cacheKey = `apod-pictures-${totalDays}`;

  try {
    const cached = sessionStorage.getItem(cacheKey);
    if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached) as ApodPicture[];
  } catch {
    // Storage unavailable or corrupted; fall through and fetch fresh data.
  }

  const pictures = await fetchFromNasa(totalDays);

  try {
    sessionStorage.setItem(cacheKey, JSON.stringify(pictures));
  } catch {
    // Caching is optional; the app still works without it.
  }

  return pictures;
}

try { ... } catch { ... } means "attempt this; if anything inside throws an error, run the catch block instead of crashing." The empty catch blocks are deliberate: there's nothing to recover, so the app simply carries on. The comments explain why for whoever reads the code next, including your grader.

Also, fetchFromNasa doesn't need its own default anymore, since fetchPictures always passes the value in. Change its first line to async function fetchFromNasa(totalDays: number): Promise<ApodPicture[]> {, so there's only one place to change the number.

Consider: 150 days is 125 requests at once

The first visit now fires 125 single-day requests simultaneously. It will probably work, but it's at the heavy end of what we discussed: a slower first load and more chance of being throttled, possibly during your demo or grading. If loading feels slow or some days come back missing, drop to 90. That still gives you three months of data and a working Month filter, with half the requests. Your call either way.

Optional: lighter thumbnails

Your thumbnails use the full-size image. Some are over 4,000 pixels wide, multiple megabytes each, displayed in a 72-pixel square. The image URLs carry size parameters, like ...M31Before_Scherer_4298.jpg?w=4298&h=3394&fit=clip.... NASA's image server might resize on request, but I haven't verified it, so test it first: open one of your image URLs in a browser, change w= to w=400 and delete the h=... part, and see whether you get a smaller image. If it works, I can show you how to request small versions for thumbnails, which would make the list and gallery load noticeably faster. If not, lazy loading is already limiting the damage.

Small notes

utils.ts has no weekday helpers, so you kept the Month filter. That's fine at 150 days, since the data spans five months, and formatMonth is still in use. Just make sure the filter shows several month buttons.

thumbnail_url in ApodPicture is never filled in by the new API. It's harmless (the ?? in getImageUrl just skips past it), so you can leave it or remove it.

The missing semicolon after API_KEY doesn't affect anything, but running the formatter (Shift + Option + F) will tidy it up along with everything else.

After the fixes, run npm run lint and npm run build, test in a new tab so you start with a fresh cache, then commit and push.
